Only on levels where you've actually killed everything that could come
across you while trapped. For monks and other kicking tanks, they're
doubly annoying when something actually wants to kill you.

That is the real secret of beartraps: they can make monsters' jobs easier
(if you're stuck in place, they'll happily zap or pincushion you), but
they can do the same for you. And unlike a pit, you can kick the loot off
them pretty easily. If you successfully make them harmless, then you can
apply them to make chokepoints or to help practice elberethlessness (while
slightly violating the spirit of the law if not the letter :).

Some initial damage could be appropriate, as would re-snapping on failure
to #untrap, but the latter would require changes to the code allowing you
to #untrap yourself while caught in the first place. The current codebase
will not allow you to do so.

(btw: I've found a nearly-foolproof way to safely defuse a beartrap. No
direct hint, but you can use the same object that fixes the other trap I
mentioned to ensure that you remain out of jaws' range during the #untrap
attempt.)


